{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Real-time visualization of MIDI Continuous Controller (CC) messages with two display modes: a scrolling time graph and current-value bars, plus performance metrics for hardware testing and technique analysis (events/s, rate, jitter).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each CC number is tracked per MIDI channel, so the same CC from different channels is displayed and measured separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Slider 1 selects the CC to monitor (0 = all CCs) and slider 6 the MIDI channel (0 = all channels): the two filters combine, so "CC1 on channel 3" is a selection like any other. Slider 3 sets the time window in seconds (1-30). Slider 7 chooses where the pair list lives: under the plot (rows take height from the graph) or beside it, as a column on the right that uses the whole window height, which is what makes a short, wide window show every pair. Metrics are computed over a 1-second window, filtered on read, so the numbers describe exactly the second they claim to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each (CC, channel) pair has its own colour shade, the bars carry it in the label, the time axis is labelled, and Freeze stops the acquisition so a reading can be examined without it moving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three numbers per pair are named after what they mean, not after how they are computed: Messages = how many times per second the controller sends; Motion = how far the value travels per second (gesture speed); Timing = how much the spacing between messages wobbles. Pointing at them says the same thing on screen.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 REAPER tools\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 My public REAPER tools, published as a {\b ReaPack repository} \u8212- install once, and REAPER keeps them up to date.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Install\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In REAPER: {\i Extensions \u8594 ? ReaPack \u8594 ? Import a repository}, paste this URL, then synchronize:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 https://github.com/solyarisoftware/reaper/raw/master/index.xml\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Manual install, if you prefer: copy the {\f1 .jsfx} {\b and} its {\f1 .jsfx-inc} companions into the same folder inside your REAPER resource directory ({\f1 Effects/<category>/} on Windows).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Packages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 | Package | ReaPack category | What it does | |---|---|---| | {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "MIDI/ccscope/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
CCScope
}}}
 | {\f1 MIDI/ccscope} | Oscilloscope for MIDI CC: scrolling graph and fader bars, per channel, with performance metrics |\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each package keeps its documentation next to its sources \u8212- README, changelog, roadmap, screenshot:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "MIDI/ccscope/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
{\f1 MIDI/ccscope/}
}}}
 \u8212- {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "MIDI/ccscope/README.md"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
README
}}}
 \u183 ? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "MIDI/ccscope/CHANGELOG.md"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
changelog
}}}
 \u183 ? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "MIDI/ccscope/TODO.md"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
roadmap
}}}
\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 How this repository is organised\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a ReaPack repository, so {\b the folder tree is the package taxonomy}: a file in {\f1 MIDI/ccscope/} is installed as {\f1 Effects/MIDI/ccscope/<file>} and appears under the {\i MIDI/ccscope} category in ReaPack. The {\f1 index.xml} at the root is generated from the files' metadata headers by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/cfillion/reapack-index"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
reapack-index
}}}
 running in GitHub Actions \u8212- do not edit it by hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each package lives in its own folder, with everything it needs: the sources, the includes it imports (which resolve relative to it), and its own documentation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Related repositories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scale collections are separate projects, each with its own documentation:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/solyarisoftware/Claude-Debussy-Scales"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Claude-Debussy-Scales
}}}
\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/solyarisoftware/Alexander-Scriabin-Scales"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Alexander-Scriabin-Scales
}}}
\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/solyarisoftware/Olivier-Messiaen-Modes"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Olivier-Messiaen-Modes
}}}
\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b IF YOU LIKE THE PROJECT, PLEASE \u11088 ?\u65039 ?STAR THIS REPOSITORY TO SHOW YOUR SUPPORT!}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A star costs nothing, and it is the only signal GitHub shows to whoever is looking for a tool like this one: the count is what makes a repository look alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If you want to go further, there is a donate button in the {\b Sponsor} menu of this repository:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.paypal.com/donate/?hosted_button_id=AEV3EVJEUFM32"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Donate with PayPal
}}}
}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is a donation, not a purchase: nothing is unlocked, nothing changes in the license, and no support is promised in exchange. The minimum is 5 EUR, for a practical reason \u8212- below that the PayPal fee takes most of it. The same button funds the other open-source work of mine (dxconvey, the scale collections), so one pointer covers all of it.\par}
